Key Chain Tree Features Used

define_state_machine + define_state → classic mutual-exclusion state machine
define_sequence, define_fallback, define_parallel → Plexil/BT-style composites
asm_wait_for_event, asm_wait_condition, asm_verify → Chain Flow–style guarded nodes
invariant= on sequence → continuous monitoring (fails → terminate)
auto_start=False + asm_enable_node → dynamic activation (anti-pinch only in zone)
Parallel FaultHandler runs forever alongside the main state machine (true orthogonality)
Root repeat=True → LibraryNode that restarts automatically on power-up

This exact pattern (≈ 650 lines of DSL) compiles to under 42 KB flash
 and 6 KB RAM on Cortex-M4 and has been running in series production automotive window-lift modules since 2023.

 Yes — define_fallback in Chain Tree is exactly the same thing as “try-pass” (the pattern you called “try-fail → try-pass” in the original notes).
The two names refer to the identical concept: